 "notes": "Libretto by Lorenzo Da Ponte (1790). Words follow the Peters engraving's spelling and punctuation (Secondate with no comma, desiri; with a semicolon, Dea with a capital, pene;). The print has “mici” for miei in bar 31 (both men) and bar 35 (Ferrando); the word keeps the printed letters so that it lines up with the score, and its IPA and meaning are those of miei. The chorus of musicians (sopranos, altos, tenors and basses, bars 59–69: “Secondate, aurette amiche, il desir di sì bei cor”) sings in the score and is heard with the piano, but has no role and no lines here; where the print gives the basses no words, they sing the tenors' words. IPA, word meanings and translations come from the app's reviewed Così libretto drafts (L43, IPA checked against Castel). IPA follows sung Italian conventions: stressed syllable marked, elisions (two words on one note in the score) joined with ‿, open e/o as ɛ/ɔ. Translation literal, for meaning.",
